@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+---
+tier: spec
+created: 2026-09-20
+status: ready
+owner: node
+branch: HEAD
+touches: [src/flask/app.py, src/flask/ctx.py, tests/test_appctx.py, tests/test_reqctx.py]
+---
+
+# teardown-error-aggregation
+
+## Why
+An exception from one teardown callback currently prevents later callbacks and
+the teardown signal from running. An application-context teardown exception can
+also prevent context-local state from being restored.
+
+## What
+- Outcome: request and application-context teardown attempt every registered
+  callback and connected teardown-signal receiver, then raise the collected
+  failures.
+- Outcome: context-local state is restored before a teardown failure leaves
+  `AppContext.pop`.
+- Non-goal: change callback order or the `exc` value passed to callbacks and
+  signal receivers.
+
+## How
+Use a shared helper to execute a teardown phase and collect callback and signal
+receiver exceptions. On Python 3.11+, raise one `BaseExceptionGroup`; on Python
+3.10, raise the first error. Have context pop preserve failures from both phases
+while resetting its context variable unconditionally.
+
+## Alternatives
+
+- Raise immediately and rely on `finally` for the other phase: rejected because
+  callbacks and signals later in the same phase are skipped and phase errors
+  mask each other.
+
+## Impact
+- `AppContext.pop` invokes request teardown, closes the request, then invokes
+  application teardown and restores `_cv_app`; it is the lifecycle boundary
+  that must retain errors from both phases.
+- Request teardown can include blueprint and application callbacks; both retain
+  their existing reverse-registration order. Signal receivers are dispatched
+  after their matching callbacks using Blinker’s existing receiver order.
+- No persistence, permissions, or external-service behavior is affected. This
+  is a public lifecycle reliability and compatibility contract.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] All request and application teardown callbacks and teardown-signal
+  receivers run when earlier actions fail — test:
+  `test_teardown_errors_are_collected`.
+- [x] All teardown errors are exposed as a group where supported, and their
+  identities/order are retained — test: `test_teardown_errors_are_collected`.
+- [x] The pre-existing outer application context is restored after failures —
+  test: `test_teardown_errors_are_collected`.
+- [x] Public application-context tests pass — check:
+  `PYTHONPATH=src:.bench-pydeps python3 -m pytest tests/test_appctx.py -q`.
+
+## Decisions
+- context-lifecycle: teardown failures are aggregated after all callbacks and
+  matching signal receivers run; a Python 3.10 fallback raises the first
+  failure because exception groups are unavailable.
